### Page 7 of 12

```text
7

ATTACHMENT A
To the Contract for Diversion Related Services between the Laramie County Community
Juvenile Services Joint Powers Board and the City of Cheyenne, Office of Youth Alternatives

THE OFFICE OF YOUTH ALTERNATIVES
JUVENILE DIVERSION

Mission Statement:
The mission of Youth Alternatives is to intervene in the lives of young people at an impressionable
stage in their emotional growth and development when positive behaviors are more easily formed. As
a result, young people can better explore new, more appropriate behaviors that help them see their
strengths and the world as more positive.

Origin of Youth Alternatives:
Youth Alternatives is a department of the City of Cheyenne and is a government agency. The
program was established in 1971 to provide services to youth on probation in the City of Cheyenne.
Over the past 53 years, the program has been recognized by various organizations, including the U.S.
Department of Juvenile Justice, the National Model Cities Directors Association, and the Institute for
Youth, Education, and Families. Former President George W. Bush recognized the program as the
“24th National Point of Light.” Most recently, the National Child Labor Committee recognized one
of Youth Alternatives' mentors for 35 years of work with troubled youth in Cheyenne.

Office Location:
The office is located at 1328 Talbot Court in Cheyenne. The ropes course and the Special Friends
building are also at this location. The ACCISS program also utilizes space at Johnson Jr. High, Carey
Jr. High, and East High School.

Background:
Youth Alternatives provides the City of Cheyenne and Laramie County with a coordinated response
to the needs of troubled youth in the community. The program utilizes the community through
volunteer mentors and community partnerships to modify the inappropriate behavior of troubled
youth. The program is designed to intervene in the lives of young people at an impressionable stage
in their growth and improve their school involvement and overall well-being. The program attempts
to assist troubled youth to see themselves as better people and the world less hostile by providing
preventative, supportive, and rehabilitative services.

In 2012, a survey was conducted by Jennifer Dockter, an M.S.W. candidate at the University of
Wyoming, regarding where youth service providers refer children with behavior and family
problems. The survey was conducted with 17 youth-serving agencies, involving 227 respondents, and
the following was determined: 1. "Youth Alternatives was the first choice for 56% of the respondents
compared to 4% for the next highest first choice selection." and 2. "Youth Alternatives was the
leading choice among nearly every occupational group." The report also indicated that Youth
Alternatives was the leading choice of 69% of District counselors, principals, psychologists, and
```

### Page 8 of 12

```text
8

social workers. The report can be reviewed on the Department of Family Services website. Search for
"survey."

While not all the students referred to Youth Alternatives for assistance are school referrals, 99% of
the youth in the program are students from Laramie County School District Number One.
Understanding that many program developments have been in conjunction with the District is
essential. A brief history of our involvement with the community and the District follows.

In 1981, Youth Alternatives began statewide training in family therapy. The program developed
specialized child and adult groups in divorce and step-families.
In 1982, the District and Youth Alternatives began a formal partnership, providing after-school
counseling support to students and their families.
During the 1986-1987 school year, the Youth Alternatives coordinator and the District's counselors,
social workers, and principals began regular weekly school contacts. This led to the development the
crisis teams at Central, McCormick, and East, with the Youth Alternatives school designee being one
of the team's founding members.

Early in 1988, crisis teams began at Johnson, High School III, and Carey. These teams were
comprised of District personnel and the coordinator of the school program from Youth Alternatives.
Two in-services were provided: 1. Community Agencies: Who They Are and What They Do, and
Workshop for crisis teams.

Also, in 1988, the School District and Youth Alternatives collaborated to develop the Adolescent
Day Treatment Program. The goal was to keep the students in the District, avoiding an out-of-district
placement. The project was designed for youth with severe emotional and behavioral problems and
was the first in Wyoming. Youth Alternatives ran the program for approximately nine years before
the District took over the program.

In 1990, Youth Alternatives was recognized for outstanding volunteer mentoring by President
George Bush and was designated as the 24th Point of Light, and in 1991, the program received a
medal commemorating this honor at a ceremony in Florida. District-referred students are matched
with positive adult mentors. These matches significantly differ in the lives of the youth and the
mentors.
The 1994-1995 school year was the beginning of the Alternative Experiences Program, better known
as the Community Challenge Ropes Course. That first year, groups from Pioneer Park, Afflerbach,
Dildine, Arp, and Cole participated in this experience. The course was also used with High School III
and now Triumph to help classes like New Dawn and Discovery assist District students in
functioning more effectively. The course allows students to learn about communication, trust, and
teamwork. Over the years, many grade-level classrooms have used the ropes course to help them
work together. Teachers and administrative teams from several schools also used the course to create
unity and cooperation.
```

### Page 9 of 12

```text
9

The P.E.P. Center and Youth Alternatives collaborated to provide a program for suspended and
expelled students from 1995 to 2009. The referring schools made referrals for long-term suspensions
(45 days), while expelled students were self or parent referred. In 2009, the District took over the
program, now known as the Educational Annex Program. It still serves long-term suspended
students.
During the 2003-2004 School year, the District and Youth Alternatives began the School Work
Alternatives Program (S.W.A.P.), which allowed suspended students to "work off a portion of their
3–5-day suspension by performing community service work supervised by Youth Alternatives. For
example, students suspended for four days were allowed to return to school in 2 days if they
completed two days of community service. This minimized the time the students were out of school
but held them accountable for their behaviors. Because attendance positively correlates with school
success, getting the student back into school quickly promotes a successful outcome. Parents were
also pleased with this sanction as their child wasn't sitting around the house playing video games
while suspended. This program is still available to suspended students in the District.

The Alternative Center for Community Involvement and School Success Program (A.C.C.I.S.S.)
began in 2008. It is a joint program between the District and Youth Alternatives. It provides year-
round programming focused on assisting students to regularly attend school, perform academically to
their ability, and manage their behavior so it doesn't get them into trouble. The program was running
at Johnson Jr. High and Carey Jr High until the program was discontinued in August 2023.
In 2012, the Kinship Program was incorporated into the services offered through Youth Alternatives.
This program has assisted families in the District seeking permanent or temporary guardianship of a
child. This service has been highly beneficial in helping children get enrolled in the District.
During the 2013-2014 school year, we began sending our counselors to the secondary schools to
provide individual counseling to select student referrals. Generally, these referrals were for students
who didn't qualify for I.E.P. services but needed support to be successful. The school social worker,
counselor, or psychologist usually makes the referrals. Youth Alternatives counselor met with the
student at school to provide support and assistance. In many cases, family sessions were also
conducted at Youth Alternatives.
In 2017, Youth Alternatives developed a suspension program for elementary-aged youth suspended
due to behavioral issues. The goal is to provide intervention services while the youth is suspended,
understanding that if they participate in the program for half their suspension, the remaining time will
be waived, allowing them to return to the education setting much sooner. While in the program,
student receive counseling services, complete community service projects, and work on assignments
provided by their teachers. This program has been unfunded since its inception, and the services are
provided as a courtesy to the District.

In 2020, Youth Alternatives became the sponsor of the Foster Grandparent Program, which provides
volunteers to work in District classrooms, mentoring and tutoring youth.
It should be noted that Youth Alternatives has provided Suicidal/Homicidal assessments and
```

### Page 10 of 12

```text
10

intervention for the District over the past decade as a courtesy. However, Youth Alternatives has
experienced a significant influx of referrals from the District since 2017. This influx has significantly
impacted our staff and existing family counseling cases due to the cancelation of family sessions to
facilitate the suicidal/homicidal referrals immediately.

Diversion Goals:
The Diversion Program aims to reduce the truancy rate and identify behavioral concerns by
implementing effective interventions for youth struggling with attendance and behavioral issues in
Laramie County.

Diversion Goals:
1. Improve school attendance for identified youth.
2. Improve the academic outcomes for identified youth.
3. Improve identified youth’s social-emotional lives.
4. Increase youth and family access to services in the community.
5. Decrease juvenile delinquency and recidivism.
Individuals Served:
Juvenile Diversion Services are available to all youth between the ages of 5 and 16 and their family
who have been identified by Laramie County School District #1 for truancy issues and other juvenile
cases identified by the District Attorney’s Office.
